  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nMain focus of the forecast this morning is the precip/convective\nchances on Wednesday and the cooldown following the first\narctic-related cold front of the season. A digging shortwave\ntrough approaches from the northwest Wednesday and boots a\nclosed low riding along the southern stream. The geometry of\nthis evolution keeps our surface level flow out of the \nsouthwest with advection from central Texas and that results in\na very moisture-starved scenario ahead of frontal passage.\nSurface dewpoints have been very consistent in the 57-58 degree\nrange which matches expectations when looking at the source\nairmass region. With that drier low level you end up with a deep\nmixed boundary layer and about 400-600 J/kg of entrainment \nfactored Mixed-Layer CAPE. The timing of solar heating and\nfrontal passage leads to HRRR/RRFSv1&2/REFS to prefer popping\nconvection across the eastern third of the area in the\nafternoon. If convection can initiate it will be amid 40-50 kts\nof deep layer flow and southwesterly 850mb winds around 30 kts.\nThis would be a fairly favorable environment for some wind \npotential with a small tornado risk (high LCLs and a fairly \nunidirectional hodograph really mitigate this though). The \nmoisture advection is a little higher at the top/just above the \nboundary layer in most high-resolution projections so I think \nits possible we get some pseudo-elevated convection even if \ncells manage to initiate.\n\nAll that rambling aside I think the most likely scenario is a\nfew isolated to scattered pseudo-elevated showers in the pre-\nfrontal environment. The contingency (maybe 2-5% probability)\ncentered around over performing moisture return or better than\nmodeled boundary layer heating would be for isolated wind or\ntornado potential with the highest chances over the Kentucky\nPennyrile.\n\nA much colder airmass invades behind the front with temperatures\nfalling 20 to 25 degrees. Highs Thursday and Friday look to be\nin the upper 40s to low 50s. Thursday still features northwest\nwinds 10-20 mph which will make it feel even chillier. We get\non the warm side of the ridge by Sunday moderating temperatures.\nECMWF/GFS show another upper shortwave tracking close by on\nMonday but for now track any precip to the south of the area as\n1034mb high pressure sets up over Ohio/Indiana.",
